# {{project.name}}

A kernel starting point for QEMU's RISC-V `virt` machine: no C library, no
startup object, and no board package.

```bash
mcpp run                              # assemble, link, and boot under qemu
mcpp build                            # image + .bin + .map + a size summary
```

## What is under this program

Nothing but the compiler and the instruction set. The manifest declines the
target's C library:

```toml
[target.riscv64-none-elf]
sysroot = ""
```

With that line the C headers leave the compile line and the C library leaves
the link. `#include <stdio.h>` stops resolving, and the image contains only
what this source tree put in it.

## The three pieces

| | |
|---|---|
| `_start` | The first instruction executed. Establishes a stack, then calls C++. In assembly because a stack must exist before any compiled function is entered. |
| `kmain` | Ordinary C++. Templates, `constexpr`, classes and references are language facilities and cost nothing here. |
| `link.ld` | Where the image is loaded, and where the stack ends. The one file that changes when the board does. |

## Growing from here

| Need | Add |
|---|---|
| `memcpy`, `memmove`, `memset`, `memcmp`, `strlen` | `mcpp add std-freestanding-nolibc` — the compiler is entitled to emit calls to the first four from code that names none of them |
| `array`, `span`, `optional`, `atomic`, `ranges` | `mcpp add std-freestanding` — the part of the standard library that needs no operating system |
| `vector`, `string`, `map` | the subset's `alloc` feature, plus an allocator this program supplies |
| A C library after all | remove the `sysroot = ""` line, and the target's own returns |

## What this template is not

`mcpp new <name> --template {{template.package.name}}` — without `:nolibc` —
gives an ordinary `int main()` above picolibc, with a heap, `printf`, and a
working `mcpp test`. That is the right starting point for firmware. This one is
for a program that intends to provide a C library rather than to consume one.
